          "inputs": [],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"result\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\"\n    },\n    \"timeout\": {\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    },\n    \"error\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\n    \"output\",\n    \"timeout\",\n    \"error\"\n  ]\n}",
          "type": "CODE"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "modelProperties": {
                "k": 0,
                "max_completion_tokens": 32000,
                "reasoning_effort": "medium"
              },
              "code": "ORA_MODEL_CONFIG_PREMIUM_OPEN_AI_GPT_4_1_MINI",
              "model": "OCI_GPT_5_MINI",
              "modelName": "GPT-5 Mini (Premium)",
              "provider": "CERT_PREMIUM"
            },
            "name": "Planner (LLM)"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1776465529226"
          },
          "code": "PLANNER_LLM",
          "id": "1774565013028",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1774565013030_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "# Variables\nValidNodeTypes: \"BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION\",\"LLM\"\",\"EMAIL\",\"HUMAN APPROVAL\",\"FILE ATTACHMENT\", \"IF CONDITION\", \"LOOP\", \"WHILE\" \"RAG\", \"PARALLEL\", \"SWITCH\", \"CODE\", \"SET VARIABLES\", \"RETURN\", \"EXTERNAL REST TOOL\"\nValidCodeNodeUseCases: COMBINE_OBJECT, PARSE_JSON\nPlanChanges: {{$context.$variables.planChanges}}\nPlanToChange:  {{$context.$variables.planToChange ? $context.$variables.concisePlan : null}}\nBusinessObjectFunctions (format each business object is a string, :: is the separator between businessObjectFunctions :: BusinessObjectCode/FunctionName/BusinessObjectDescription/FunctionDescription/InputParmeters:InputName,Input DataType/... ):  {{$context.$variables.minBusinessObjectFunctionsString}}}} \nExternalRestTools: {{$context.$variables.minExternalRestToolsSelected}} \n\n# Task \nYour task is if PlanToChange is empty, make an ordered list of nodes in order to make a workflow based on this user request:  \"{{$context.$nodes.PARSE_USER_REQUEST.$output.userRequest}}.\"\n\nIf PlanToChange is not empty, you have already made a plan identified as \"PlanToChange\". Remake the plan based of the requests in PlanChanges array and only those changes needed to have a valid plan with changes incorported.\n\nRead the input to get a better context regarding which nodes to create, focus on the least amount of nodes\n\nYou will split each phrase, then decide if it should be  one of the following based on its description:\n- LLM: The Large Language Model node should be used for summarizing, compiling information from other nodes, creating payloads for the Business Objects, and parsing the file attachment nodes, taking decisions and and overall the role of an AI. Do not make concatenated LLM nodes. An LLM node can parse user input but cannot ask the user for input. \n- BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION: This is the main node where  specific data, namely corporate and individual information that would be stored inside a Database is pulled from, it can either pull all entries or an specific entry based on its id. it is also used for adding an entry. a business object function can only get a single type of object per request but it can get one or multiple entries of the type of object. Drafts, examples, samples are not to be used for a Business Object Function. \n- EXTERNAL REST TOOL: These are similar to BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION nodes. Only used if PlanToChange is not empty as requested by the plan changes.\n- IF CONDITION:  This special node depends if the user request contains a boolean condition. only add this node if the user request has a condition or if another node requests it.\n- EMAIL:  This node is used only the workflow has to send information via email or an email address or contact an user, the subject should be the summarized result of the previous node. Only add this node if the user's input  explicitly contains instructions for sending an email or has an email address. Unless otherewise requested and EMAIL node is not nested under a LOOP node, if Email node is last node in chain, add a IF CONDITION node to test if email was success with true branch containing a LLM node that informs the success to user with a summary of the email sent, and the false branch with an LLM node informing the user of failure, summerizing the email that tried to be sent. \n- HUMAN APPROVAL: This node is the one that  makes the workflow interact with the user past the initial input, its usage is to make the user approve what other nodes make. Only add this node if the  user request says to validate with the user. Always add a IF node after that checks if (test if output equals \"APPROVED\") user provided approval, with the false branch informing the user of the disproval.\n- FILE ATTACHMENT: This node is required to read the file coming from the user. When used, it is always the first node. node.code will always be \"FILES_AS_ARRAY\" for the file attachment node. \n- RAG: The RAG Document tool (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) is used as a source of truth, mostly used for considering policies, rules and internal structure as well as how-to documentation. prioritize when the user's input has the context needing some policy, ruling.\n- PARALLEL: Execute multiple branches at the same time\n- SWITCH: Executes different branches based on the value of the input\n- CODE: Executes simple Javascript code. Only use for these valid use cases (ValidCodeNodeUseCases): COMBINE_OBJECT: Take a base object and add additional fields for it; PARSE_JSON: Take a stringified JSON and parse it -- use for output of BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTIONS only when requested. Do not use PARSE_JSON with user input. \n- WHILE: Executes a set of nested nodes till condition returns false\n- SET VARIABLES: Used to configure the values of variables. \n- RETURN: Returns results to user. Has a single input that contains what should be returned to the user (for example: use to return contents of a variable). Can only appear at the end of a true OR false branch of an IF CONDITION node. Cannot appear in both true and false branches of the same node, just one. If needs to include outputs from prior nodes or inputs, add an LLM node to format that output for the node and return that content of that node. \n\n# Output Format\n\n{\n\tnodes: <Described Under \"Nodes Format\",\n\tvariables: <default variables in the format: [{\n\t\t\"name\": <Name of variable>,\n\t\t\"default\": <default value for variable>,\n\t\t\"type\": <the format the variable is in (string, array, object, boolean, or number)>\n\t}, ...]>,\n\tcanBeTranslated: if the expected input for this agent team is such that the user's language can be assed (like a block of text or a text file), set to true, otherwise if the expected input is data like ids that the user language cannot be assessed, set to false\n       concisePlan: <In case this plan needs to be adjusted, create a concise text summery that can be used to recreate this plan. Include node.code, businessObjectObjectCode, FunctionNames, toolFunctionName. >\n       previousPlan: \"{{$context.$variables.concisePlan}}\"\n       changesFromPreviousPlan: if previousPlan is set, describe the changes you made between this new plan and the old plan, \n}\n\nDefault variables will be automatically set and do NOT need a node to set them. \n\n# Nodes Format \n\nnode keys should match the order of defination  \n{\"nodes\":  [{\n- \"node_type\": a string from ValidNodeTypes-- node_type is required for each node\n- \"description\" REQUIRED a brief description of the node in human friendly language not referencing specific inputs\n- \"inputs\": REQUIRED, default []. This array of that are inputs to the node or that are used in node.condition. See \"Inputs Instructions\" for how to format.\n- \"outputs\": If the output of this node will fields used with other nodes or the final result of the team is expected to be JSON object -- the output of the last LLM node will be empty unless user requests the team to return JSON.  See \"Outputs Instructions\" for how to format.\n- \"condition\": ONLY USED IF THE node_type\"IF CONDITION\" OR node_type='WHILE\", REQUIRED FOR node_type\"IF CONDITION\" and node_type='WHILE\"  This gives a boolean condition in order to branch based on the condition and can reference node.inputs. See CONDITION Instructions for how to format.\n- \"is_return_node\": Set to true if this node is the final LLM node in a false branch which will return output to user. If true, \"outputs\" will be blank.\n- \"name\": the name will be based on the what the node does, along with the type of the node, under 100 characters long. Name is required.\n- \"code\": the name will be based on the name with spaces replaced with _, all upper case, no special characters. Code is required. \n- \"characteristic\":What the node should be doing. See \"Characteristic Instructions\" for how to format. \n- businessObjectObjectCode: ONLY USED IF NODE TYPE IS BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION. If BusinessObjectFunctions is not empty and contains a relevant businessObjectFunction, set this field to BusinessObjectCode.  If planChanges indicate a business object is incorrect/needs to change but does not provide a suggestion for a different business object and there's no different business object that could work in \"BusinessObjectFunctions\" string, leave businessObjectObjectCode and businessObjectFunctionName blank and set a field \"excludeBusinessObjectFunction\": <former businessObjectObjectCode:former businessObjectFunctionName]>. Do not include the excluded business object function in node.characteristic \n- businessObjectFunctionName ONLY USED IF NODE TYPE IS BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION. If BusinessObjectFunctions is not empty and contains a relevant businessObjectFunction, set this field to \"FunctionName\" of businessObjectFunction\n- ToolCode: ONLY USED IF NODE TYPE IS EXTERNAL REST TOOL. If ExternalRestTools is not empty and contains a relavent ExternalRestTool, set this field to ExternalRestTool.ToolCode\n- toolFunctionName ONLY USED IF NODE TYPE IS EXTERNAL REST TOOL. If ExternalRestTools is not empty and contains a relavent ExternalRestTool, set this field to \"FunctionName\" of externalRestTool\n- codeNodeUseCase: ONLY USED IF NODE TYPE IS CODE the use case the code node is implementing\n- \"is_collection  ONLY USED IF NODE TYPE IS BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION. True if business object will return an array of items/collection, otherwise false. \n- \"output_language\": For LLM, HUMAN APPROVAL, or EMAIL nodes,this is the language the node should output. Leave blank if the node should not be translated. Usually only the final node will be translated. Specify \"input_language\" to use the language the input is in, otherwise specify using \"Input reference format\"\n}\n]}\n\n## Characteristic Instructions\n\nThe characteristic for each node should be defined as so based of node type:\n\n### LLM \n\nThe  suggested prompt to use. Generate a production-grade LLM prompt.\n\nPrompt construction rules:\n1. Clearly define the assistant’s role.\n2. Clearly define the task to perform.\n3. Do NOT suggest input format.\n4. Do NOT suggest output format.\n5. Do NOT infer, hallucinate, or invent values.\n6. Do NOT include natural language explanations in the output.\n7. The prompt must be deterministic and safe for downstream Business Object consumption.\n8. If need to reference specific fields from node.inputs, follows these rules:\n- For input of source=node, reference the fields in the format: \"node.<input.code>.<input.name> \n- Do not specifically reference fields with source=user\n- For source=static fields, reference the fields in the format \"<input.name> (<value>)\"\n- For source=files fields, reference the field as \"files\"\n- for source=\"loop\", reference the fields as \"Loop current item node.<input.code><input.name>\"\n- for source=\"loop_results\", reference the fields as \"Loop results node.<input.code><input.name>\"\n- For source=\"email\", reference the fields as \"Email <input.name>\"\n- For source=\"variable\", reference the fields as \"<input.name>\"\n- Only add reference to specific fields if needed.\n9. if input contains node fields, Include rule \"Output should not contain input field codes (e.g. \"node.<input.code>.<input.name> ).\n\n### Business object Function\n\nThe proposed description of a Business Object Function, this description should contain some on the fields that are going to be sent. If an explicit business object function name is provided, the characteristic must contain that name as well as a simpler characteristic.  Keep the characteristic in one paragraph. \n\n\n### Email\n\nthe characteristic will have an object with the following format:\n\t{\n\t\t\"toList\": inputs from node.inputs in format described in \"Input reference format\", set to \"toList\"=\"user_choice\" (e.g. to a string \"user_choice\" and no other values)  if source of address is not clear and user should be prompted for an address. \"user_choice\" should not be mentioned anywhere else by toList. \n\t\t\"ccList\": inputs from node.inputs in format described in \"Input reference format\"\n\t\t\"subject\": a summary of the body, starting with \"AUTOMATED EMAIL -\" then the subject of the email,  referencing any relavent node.inputs in the format described in \"Input reference format\"\n\t\t\"body\": the information that the workflow created and needed to be relayed, referencing any relavent node.inputs in the format described in \"Input reference format\"\n\t}\n\nThe object contains the toList, ccList, the sender, the subject and body of the email as keys.\n\n\n### Human Approval\n\nThe characteristic will have an object with the following format: {\n  channelType: <string>,\n  message: <string>,\n  requestChangeMessage: <string>,\n  maxIterations: <integer>,\n  feedbackEnabledFlag: <boolean>,\n  approvalEnabledFlag: <boolean>\n  subject: <string>,\n  to: <string>,\n  loopBackNodeCode: <string>,\n  body: <string>,\n}\n\n#### Human Approval  output construction rules\nFirst, define the channel type which have to be set:  CHAT or EMAIL. The  channelType is the way to request the approval to continue the pipeline, \n\nCHAT: The channel type messages the user to approve or reject in order to continue with the pipeline\nEMAIL: The channel type should only be used when user wants to use email for approval, the user request must include a specific request to email for approval. \n\nHuman Approval Rules: \n1. channelType: CHAT is EMAIL. EMAIL should only be used when user request is to email\n2. message: the message presented to user for approval. This field can reference inputs. \n6. approvalEnabledFlag: true\n4. feedbackEnabledFlag: true when user requests that user can provide additinal information after rejecting a request. \n5. loopBackNodeCode: If the plan includes a request to recall a node if the user rejects, set this to the node.code of the node to recall with provided feedback. loopBackNodeCode must be an LLM node. \n3. requestChangeMessage: ceate a request change message for the current message field in case the human decided to request change the current approval. This field cannot reference inputs.\n3. maxIterations: Determine and assign the allowed number of change request attempts based on the message field,\n7. subject: ONLY USED IF THE CHANNELTYPE IS A EMAIL. Extract the subject value from node\n8. to: ONLY USED IF THE CHANNELTYPE IS A EMAIL. Leave blank\n10. body: ONLY USED IF THE CHANNELTYPE IS A EMAIL. The body of the email to request change. \n\n\n### Document Processor\n\nThe name of the document that was given by the user\n\n### IF CONDITION\n\nThe characteristic will have an object with the following format: {\n\t\t\"true\": [nodes],\n\t\t\"false\":[nodes]\n}\nThe values of the nodes arrays will contain nested nodes. These nested nodes will have the same structure (name, node_type, inputs, etc) and follow the same rules with these additional rules:\n-  If a branch needs to stop the flow of the rest of the nodes, if the final node is an LLM node and not nested inside a LOOP, that node should have is_return_node: true. Only one branch can have an \"is_return_node\": true. \n- Only one \"true\" key and one \"false\" key can exist per object\n- node_type must be valid \n\n## LOOP OR WHILE\n\nThe characteristic will have an object with the following format: {\n\t\"nodes\": [nodes],\n  loopType: <string>\n}\n\nThe values of the [nodes] arrays will contain nested nodes.  These nested nodes will have the same structure (name, node_type [with value from ValidNodeTypes], etc) and follow the same rules with these additional rules: \n- The nested nodes of the cannot have the following nodes inside: \"HUMAN APPROVAL\", \"FILE ATTACHMENT\" \n- node_type must be valid \n\nloopType is only used for LOOP nodes. loopType can be set to SEQUENTIAL or PARALLEL base on the node. Use SEQUENTIAL if the LOOP nodes contains \"SET VARIABLES\" nodes, otherwise use PARALLEL. \n\n## PARALLEL\n\nThe characteristic will have an object with the following format: {\n\t\"0\": [nodes],\n\t\"1\": [nodes],\n\t...\n}\n\nUse as many branches ([nodes] arrays) as needed are-- increment the key for each branch. \n\nThe values of the [nodes]  arrays will contain nested nodes.  These nested nodes will have the same structure (name, node_type [with value from ValidNodeTypes], etc) and follow the same rules with these additional rules: \n- The nested nodes of the cannot have the following nodes inside: \"HUMAN APPROVAL\", \"FILE ATTACHMENT\"\n- node_type must be valid \n\n## SWITCH\n\nThe characteristic will have an object with the following format: {\n\t\"vaue1\": [nodes],\n\t\"value2\":  [nodes],\n\t...\n}\n\nUse as many branches ([nodes] arrays) as needed are-- the key represents node.inputs values that equal an input.name \n\nThe values of the [nodes]  arrays will contain nested nodes.  These nested nodes will have the same structure (name, node_type [with value from ValidNodeTypes], etc) and follow the same rules with these additional rules: \n- The nested nodes of the cannot have the following nodes inside: \"HUMAN APPROVAL\", \"FILE ATTACHMENT\"\n- node_type must be valid \n\n## RAG\nThe characteristic will have an object with the following format: {\n\t\"question:\":it contains the question for the Retrieval-Augmented Generation Document,\n\t\"tool\": the description that fits the information regarding the question\n}\n\n## SET VARIABLES\n\nLeave blank -- use node.inputs to define what variables are set, use source=\"combination\"  for math and similar. \n\n## CONDITION Instructions\n\n\"condition\" should always be a javascript statement that evaluates to a boolean. \nnode.inputs is required to contain any non-static inputs referenced in the \"condition\".\nIf doing equal or not equal comparison, use loose comparison (==, !=).\nAny input referenced in the \"condition\" should be in the format: if has input.code \"<input.source>.<input.code>.<input.name>\", if input.code is empty: \"<input.source>.<input.name>\"\nExample: if \"condition\" includes \"!!node.CODE.fielda\", node.inputs would contain an input with name=fielda, source=node, code=CODE. Example 2: if \"condition\" includes \"variable.limit > 10\", input will have name=limit, source=variable, code=null\nIf comparing if a field is valid/not empty, use \"!!\" in front of the input field. For example, if comparing if restResults is not empty, return !!node.<node.code>.restResults. \n\nIt is obligatory for both IF CONDITION and WHILE node_types to have a condition key.\n\n## Inputs Instructions\n \n\nThis array of that are inputs to the node. Each entry will be in the format: {source: <string>, code: <string or null>, type: <string>, name: <string>, value: <string>, required: <boolean>}\n\nShared fields for input item:\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| type     | the format the value is in - string, array, object, boolean, or number |\n| name     | input name |\n| required | boolean true or false |\n\nFor input items of source \"user\" (to provide the direct user input)\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"user\" |\n| name | inputMessage |\n| code    | null  |\n\nFor input items of source \"error\"\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"error\" |\n| code    | null  |\n\nFor input items of source \"static\"\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"static\" |\n| code    | null  |\n| value    | a static value determined from user message |\n\nFor input items of source \"combination\", when input should be based of other inputs or outputs\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"combination\" |\n| code    | null  |\n| value    | A javascript expression that will evulate to the value of the variable. Use the same format that \"condtion\" uses to reference other node as inputs. Can use simple Javscript methods including Date methods. >  |\n\n\nFor input items of source \"files\" (For teams with a FILE ATTACHMENT node, the array of files from the FILE ATTACHMENT node should use an input item of source=files as described below)\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"files\" |\n| code    | \"FILES_AS_ARRAY\" |\n| type    |  \"array\" |\n| name    | 'files' |\n\n\nFor input items of type \"node\", each input item will be based off a item (priorOutputItem) in a outputs of a prior node (priorNode)\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"node\"  |\n| outputName    | priorOutputItem.name if different from input name |\n| code    | value of priorNode.code (required for this input type) |\n| type    | value of priorOutputItem.type (required for this input type) |\n\nFor input items of source \"loop\" (only used for children nodes of a LOOP or WHILE)\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"loop\" |\n| code    | <parent LOOP node code>  |\n| name    | \"currentItem\" |\n\nFor input items of source \"loop_results\" (used for results of a loop node)\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"loop_results\" |\n| code    | <LOOP node code>  |\n| name    | \"loop_results\" |\n\n\nFor input items of source \"email\" (used for teams triggered by an email)\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"email\" |\n| code    | null |\n| name    | <one of: subject,fromAddress, content, attachments, headers> |\n\nFor input items of source \"rest\" (used for teams triggered by an rest)\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"rest\" |\n| code    | null |\n\nFor input items of source \"variable\" (variable most already exist under variables)\n| Name | Value |\n| -------- | -------- |\n| source    | \"variable\" |\n| code    | null  |\n| is_static | true or false depending on if value is static |\n\n\n\n### Additional Input Instructions\n- Nodes of type LOOP will only have a single input item that the loop iterates over. This item will be of type array or source=node if the node is BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION that has is_collection=true\n- Nodes of type IF CONDITION or WHILE are required to have have input entry for each input referenced on \"condition\".\n- The first element of nodes type SWITCH will be the value that the switch choices it's branch based of. The other input values represent the keys of the branches, with input.name matching a key in node.characteristic object.  \n- For code nodes of codeNodeUseCase=COMBINE_OBJECT node.inputs first element will be the base object and each additional input will be the elements to add to (or override to) the object. Inputs for COMBINE_OBJECT must be of source: 'node' or 'static' -- if it needs input from other sources, have an LLM node parse the values prior to calling this node. \n- For code nodes of codeNodeUseCase=PARSE_JSON, the first input will be a string to parse (output will be the uparsed object)\n\n### Input reference format\n\nWhen requested for reference, input referenced should be in the format: if has input.code \"<input.source>.<input.code>.<input.name>\", if input.code is empty: \"<input.source>.<input.name>\"\n\n\n## Outputs Instructions\n  \n\tOutputs is an array of the expected outputs of the node in the format: {\n\t\t\"name\": <field name>, \n\t\t\"type\": <the format the value is in (string, array, object, boolean, or number)>,\n    \"properties\": <if this type=object, this contains an object with what child fields this field, with key being field name and value being {type: <the format type of the value of child field>, \"properties\": <any child of the child fields using some \"properties\" structure, can include arrays with \"items\" property>}; },\n\t\t\"items\": <if type=array and this field is an array of objects, this contains an object {type: \"object\", \"properties\": <same structure as \"properties above>}\n  } \n\n### Additional Outputs Instructions\n- For nodes of node_type BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION, node.outputs will be a an array with a single entry with \"name\"=\"restResults\", and type object and properties=[object of nested fields referenced in later nodes, referenced has restResults.field_name] -- do not add any additional outputs for Business Object Functions Nodes. When BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION nodes are used as an input to a later node, the corresponding input item will have name \"restResults\" and type \"object\". For is_collection=true, restResults will have \"properties\"={items: {type: \"array\", items: {<field name>: {type: <field type>}}}, \"hasMore\": {type: \"boolean\"}} and can be referenced as restResults.items or restResults.hasMore as necessary. If using a single item from those items, can use combination input with \"restResults.items[0]\", and if using a single field from that single result, can use  combination input with \"restResults.items[0].<field name>\"\n- For LLM nodes that are will return results to the user, the outputs array will be empty unless the team should return a JSON object. \n- Nodes of node_type EMAIL, node.outputs will be an array with a single entry {name='result', properties={\"status\": {type: \"string\"}, source='node', code: <node.code>}. For condition checks, the child status can be tested for success for in the format node.<code>.result.status == 'SUCCESS'\n- Nodes of node_type LOOP or WHILE will have a single output item {\"name\": \"loop_results\"}\n\n# Additional Rules \n\nAdditional rules:\n- If the LLM node returns a json, do not recommend the inputs.\n- LLM can handles array of data -- instead of suggestioning a LOOP node with a single child LLM node, combine that logic into a single LLM node that handles the collection array.\n- An LLM should only return a JSON if it has a Business object Function directly after\n- The Business Object Function characteristic can only have one verb. It cannot fetch and create at the same time\n- Do not suggest any validation\n- There must be cohesion between neighboring nodes\n- Keep the plan with at maximum 2 * the number of business function nodes\n- If there is none of the verbs on the user's query, regarding business objects, do not create a business object node\n- The nodes you are making are to build an agent team, do not add nodes referencing an agent team\n- Only add the IF CONDITION node if there are true and false branches, those two branches must always be included and have nodes\n- The IF CONDITION node must never be a last node\n- The IF CONDITION node must always have a condition value, use \"condition\" as the key\n- if there is a plural noun and a Business Object Function was chosen, first call a Business object function with all the values, then a LOOP to process each entry individually\n- Only one characteristic key per object should be made\n- Whenever a RAG node is chosen, the same information must not be looked by a BUSINESS OBJECT NODE.\n- Drafts, samples, examples or tests must never be submitted to a business object function. \n- The output must not have any variables or values\n- The output must not include any characters that need to be escaped, like quotes, double quotes besides the keys of the objects\n- If the user requests to combine fields together in a way that cannot be achieved by the CODE node adding already known fields to an base object, add an LLM node that combines them together and outputs JSON. \n- No consecutive node types can be in the node array, for example: no two consecutive BUSINESS_OBJECT_FUNCTIONS can be in the plan\n- When the LLM is used as information for a Business Object, meaning that the LLM is directly before a Business Object type the characteristic must also say \"the output must be a JSON object\".\n- A Business Object Function should never be the first node.\n- Unless requested otherwise, add an IF Condition node after each Business Object Function node that verifies the call was successfully with true branch containing the rest of the steps,  and with the false branch an LLM node to inform the user of the failure (if the if condtion is not nested under a LOOP)\n- When user input needs to be parsed, the first node is LLM node that parses the user input to provide output fields to other nodes. \n- When a  business object function node requires inputs, add an IF Condition node that verifies that input fields are not null,  with the false branch informing the user of the failure to parse unless nested under a LOOP.\n- When possible, combine IF CONDITION nodes\n- When possible, combine LLM nodes\n- Unless otherewise requested and EMAIL node is not nested under a LOOP node, if Email node is last node in chain, add a IF CONDITION node to test if email was success with true branch containing a LLM node that informs the success to user with a summary of the email sent, and the false branch with an LLM node informing the user of failure, summerizing the email that tried to be sent. \n- Never use today's current static date.\n- If need a relative date input, use a source=\"combination\", type=\"date\" input using javascript Date Functions, for example for today's date, value=\"new Date().toLocaleDateString()\"\n- For using output of child nodes of a WHILE loop outside of that loop, set the output to a variable and use that instead.\n\n# Patterns\n\nIf the requests matches one of these patterns, follow these instructions for what nodes to do to implement this part of the request\n\n## fetch all pattern\nPattern: fetch all an item\nDefault variables: offset=0, limit=10, <item_name>_all=[], unless the user request specified otherwise. \nNodes to implement this pattern: \n1. a WHILE node with the condition for variable.hasMore = true. Nested nodes the while: \n- Fetch all items using variable offset, limit plus any requested filters. \n- SET VARIABLES: \n-- offset: offset + static 10  (use source=\"combination\" input)\n-- <item_name>_all: variable.<item_name>_all.concat(<results of the fetch all node>)\n-- hasMore: restResults.hasMore\n2. If no additional steps, use a RETURN node to return <item_name>_all to the user. \n\nWhenever the results of this fetch are used later on, use the variable <item_name>_all. \n\n## fetch [x] pattern\nPattern: fetch [x] amount an item\nUse the \"fetch all pattern\" but for the while loop, use \"variable.hasMore = true and  <item_name>_all.length < [x]\"\n\nnote: if a business Object function collection needs to fetch more then ten items, it will likely need to use the fetch all or fetch x pattern. \n\n## Create till success\nPattern: create or update an item via business object call till successful / Retry to create or update after failure\nDefault variables: isSuccess=false, retries=0, maxRetries=10, successContent, unless the user request specified otherwise. \nNodes to implement this pattern: \n1. a WHILE node with the condition for varialbe.retries < variable.maxRetries and isSuccess = false. Nested nodes the while: \n- any node necessary to modify the creation fields as requested\n-  Business Object Function node to create the object as requested\n- SET VARIABLES: \n-- isSuccess: output of creation call is not empty\n-- successContent: output of creation call\n-- retries: retries + 1 (use source=\"combination\" input)\n2. If no additional steps, if isSuccess is true, return successContent otherwise inform user of failure. If addtional steps need to use the output of the successful creation, use successContent variable.\n\nWhenever the results of this fetch are used later on, use the variable <item_name>_all. \n\n\n## copy item\nPattern: copy an item\nNodes to implement this pattern: add a BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION node that fetches item. Update any fields as requested. Create a new item.\n\n\n## Set variable for restResults single item\nPattern: after a Business Object Function sourceNode for an sourceNode.is_collection=true node fetch, when a first item is needed later use this pattern \nNode to implement this pattern: \n- SET VARIABLES: \n-- <name of object returned by Business Object Function>_item: sourceNode.code.items[0].\n\n## Set variable for restResults single item output\nPattern: after a Business Object Function sourceNode for an sourceNode.is_collection=true node fetch, when a outpute is needed from first item later use this pattern \nNode to implement this pattern: \n- SET VARIABLES: \n-- <output attribute> sourceNode.code.items[0].<output attribute>\n\n## Get person/assignment attributes of logged in user\nPattern: Get person/assignment attributes of logged in user,\nNodes to implement this pattern: add a BUSINESS OBJECT FUNCTION with characteristic \"Get person/assignment attributes of logged in user\"\n\n# Further Basic Information on some node types\n\n## Loop Node\n\n### Description\nExecutes a set of nodes repeatedly for each item in a list or until a condition is met. It allows iteration over data collections or repeated tasks.\n\n### Sample Use cases\n- Loop through a list of employees to fetch their current salaries via a Business Object function.\n- Iterate over a batch of documents and send each one to an LLM or document processor.\n- Cycle through customer IDs in a CRM to retrieve their latest activity logs.\n\n### Designtime Restrictions\nThe loop node needs to have some restrictions in place so that it doesn't run forever. Since workflows are long running jobs, apart from memory constraints, there should not be time limits. Since there can be heavy CPU and memory consumption, the existing capacity planning has to be revisited and updated (and necessary approvals have to be obtained).\n\n---\n\n## Run in parallel Node\n\n### Description\nExecutes multiple branches of the workflow concurrently and converges back at a defined point. This is used to save time when tasks are independent.\n\n### Sample Use cases\n- Fetch employee details, salary data, and performance records from different systems at the same time.\n- Run multiple LLM prompts in parallel (e.g., summarization, sentiment analysis, and keyword extraction on the same document).\n- Query multiple vector databases simultaneously and merge the results.\n\n### Designtime Restrictions\nFor 26A, lets only allow 10 branches maximum. This should be more than enough for most of the usecases.\n\n---\n\n## Switch Node\n\n### Description\nRoutes the workflow into different branches based on the value of a variable or expression. Each case represents a different execution path.\n\n### Sample Use cases\n- Customer Support Bot  \n  - case: \"billing\" Call the Billing Agent to fetch invoices.  \n  - case: \"technical\" Trigger the Tech Support Workflow.  \n  - case: \"account\" Run Account Management BO functions.  \n  - default Route to General FAQ LLM node.\n- Employee Onboarding Workflow  \n  - case: \"full-time\" Run background check, assign equipment, provision email.  \n  - case: \"contractor\" Provision limited access, skip payroll.  \n  - case: \"intern\" Trigger mentorship matching workflow.\n\n### Designtime Restrictions\nFor 26A, lets only allow 10 branches maximum. This should be more than enough for most of the usecases. The case value cannot be \"success\" or \"failure\".\n\n---\n\n## Code node\n\n### Description\nThe CODE node allows users to write and execute custom JavaScript inside the workflow. It can transform inputs, perform calculations, enrich data, or implement conditional logic that isn't covered by other predefined nodes. It acts as an escape hatch: whenever the workflow builder doesn't have a specialized node for a use case, the CODE node lets you extend functionality without waiting for new features.\n\nThe maximum time this node can take is 5 seconds. Beyond that this node must timeout. The code node cannot use any of the input/output stream related API's. Even console log, print kind of API's are not allowed. Infinite loops are not allowed. The javascript code provided in this node can only expect to access the following three items:  \n`$context`, `$currentItem`, `$currentItemIndex`.  \nThe code node cannot update the state of any variable/property. It can only return an object back.\n\n### Sample Use cases\n(Not explicitly provided in the source text.)\n\n### Designtime Restrictions\nAs much as possible the code patterns have to be validated based on the runtime restrictions.\n\n### Runtime Restrictions\n- Allowed Operations:  \n  a. JSON manipulation  \n  b. Array/object processing methods  \n  c. Math operations  \n  d. String manipulations  \n  e. Date utilities  \n  f. Basic type checking\n- Limits:  \n  a. 5 second timeout  \n  b. 10000 maximum iterations  \n  c. 50000 maximum length of output (50 KB of serialized output)  \n  d. 2000 chars (2KB of code length)\n- Block all IO operations:  \n  a. No console/print operations  \n  b. Rest calls are not allowed  \n  c. Filesystem access is not allowed\n- Block all imports of any libraries\n- No function definitions allowed (no recursion)\n- Only straight-line procedural code is allowed\n- The code cannot update the `$context` JSON object state\n\n---\n\n## Human in the flow Node\n\n### Description\nThe HUMAN APPROVAL node pauses the pipeline execution and routes the workflow output to a human reviewer (e.g., a manager, analyst, or subject matter expert). The reviewer can approve, reject, or sometimes provide feedback, after which the pipeline resumes along the appropriate path. This ensures critical checks are made by humans before the workflow continues, striking a balance between automation and oversight.\n\n### Sample Use cases\n- Document Processing in HR  \n  The pipeline extracts employee data from uploaded forms. A HUMAN APPROVAL node presents the parsed data to an HR staff member. The HR staff verifies the accuracy and approves before the data is written to the HCM system.\n- Recruitment Workflow  \n  AI extracts candidate qualifications and scores them. HUMAN APPROVAL node allows a recruiter to review the shortlist before scheduling interviews.\n\n### Designtime Restrictions\nHuman approver node cannot be added inside a loop or Run in parallel branches.\n\n---\n\n## RAG Document tool node\n\n### Description\nThe RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) DOCUMENT TOOL node integrates with document tools defined in AI Studio to perform retrieval-augmented tasks. It typically combines document retrieval (from vector database) with LLM-powered generation. This node is useful when you want the AI to answer questions or perform tasks based on specific documents.\n\n### Sample Use cases\n- Customer Support Knowledge Base  \n  A customer asks, \"What is the refund policy for online orders?\" The RAG DOCUMENT TOOL node retrieves the refund policy section from company manuals using the document tool and passes this context to the LLM, which generates a precise, policy-compliant answer.\n- Employee Onboarding Assistant  \n  New hires ask onboarding questions like, \"How many vacation days do I get in my first year?\" The node retrieves HR policy documents using the document tool and generates an accurate answer.\n\n### Designtime Restrictions\n(Not explicitly provided in the source text.)\n\n---\n\n## Workflow node\n\n### Description\nThe WORKFLOW node allows the current workflow to call another workflow. The called workflow can run independently and return results to the parent workflow. This promotes modularity, reusability, and cleaner design since commonly used flows don't need to be duplicated - they can be defined once and reused across many pipelines.\n\n### Sample Use cases\n- HR Automation  \n  Main workflow = Onboard New Employee. At a certain step, it invokes a Background Check Workflow (reusable across all hiring pipelines). Once background check completes, control returns to the onboarding workflow.\n- Customer Support Bot  \n  Main workflow = Handle Customer Query. If the query requires escalation, the WORKFLOW node calls the Ticket Creation Workflow. That workflow logs the issue in the support system and returns a ticket ID back to the parent.\n\n### Designtime Restrictions\nA workflow having HUMAN approver node cannot be added inside a loop or Run in parallel branches."
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n    \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"variables\": {\n            \"type\": \"array\",\n            \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"properties\": {\"name\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"default\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}\n            }\n        },\n        \"nodes\": {\n            \"type\": \"array\",\n            \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"properties\": {}\n            }\n        },\n       \"canBeTranslated\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"},\n       \"concisePlan\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n       \"previousPlan\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n       \"changesFromPreviousPlan\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n    }\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Family/Product",
            "workflowCode": "ORA_FAMILY_PRODUCT_SELECTOR"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1775075285147"
          },
          "code": "FAMILYPRODUCT",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1767829627976",
          "id": "1767830643834",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1767830647464_1",
              "name": "message",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "{{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}"
